                "rationale": "A form with many bindings or clauses is doing many things in one place. Splitting it — into smaller lets, a dispatch table, or separate functions per clause — usually makes each piece independently readable and testable, at the cost of naming the pieces.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a let/let*/cond/case-family form carrying more bindings or clauses than a threshold"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "defvar and defparameter both mean the value may change over the program's lifetime. When nothing in the defining file ever assigns to one again, that possibility is unused here, and defconstant says the same binding more precisely."
              },
              "id": "effectively-constant-variable",
              "properties": {
                "category": "naming",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "defvar and defparameter both mean the value may change over the program's lifetime. When nothing in the defining file ever assigns to one again, that possibility is unused here, and defconstant says the same binding more precisely.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": [
                  "pedantic"
                ]
