            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When the datum of `error`/`signal`/`warn`/`cerror` is a symbol naming a condition type, the remaining arguments are alternating initarg names and values — not format arguments. A string in an initarg-name position is either an `odd-length initializer list` error, which means the named condition is never signalled, or, with an even argument count, an unknown initarg that is silently discarded. SBCL diagnoses neither at compile time."
              },
              "id": "condition-type-datum-with-string-initarg",
              "properties": {
                "category": "conditions",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "When the datum of `error`/`signal`/`warn`/`cerror` is a symbol naming a condition type, the remaining arguments are alternating initarg names and values — not format arguments. A string in an initarg-name position is either an `odd-length initializer list` error, which means the named condition is never signalled, or, with an even argument count, an unknown initarg that is silently discarded. SBCL diagnoses neither at compile time.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a signalling form naming a condition type and then passing a string, which lands in an initarg-name position rather than being a message"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Cleanup forms run during the unwind, so an `error` signalled there replaces the condition that caused the unwind. The original is not chained or logged — the caller is told about the cleanup's failure and never hears about the one that started it."
              },
              "id": "unwind-protect-cleanup-signals",
              "properties": {
                "category": "conditions",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Cleanup forms run during the unwind, so an `error` signalled there replaces the condition that caused the unwind. The original is not chained or logged — the caller is told about the cleanup's failure and never hears about the one that started it.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an unwind-protect cleanup form that calls error, replacing whatever condition was unwinding"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "LFE inherits Erlang's guard restriction: a guard may only call a fixed set of BIFs and operators. `lfe_lint.erl` rejects anything else with `illegal guard expression`, and its `check_gexpr/4` permits a qualified call in exactly one case — module `erlang`, a function in the guard BIF set, at an arity that set allows. Every other module-qualified call in a guard is a compile error, not a style question. Verified against LFE 2.2.0 on OTP 27.3.4.15: `(lists:member x '(1 2))` and `(call 'lists 'member x '(1 2))` in a guard both fail to compile, while `(erlang:is_atom x)` compiles cleanly."
              },
              "id": "lfe-illegal-guard-call",
              "properties": {
                "category": "malformed",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "LFE inherits Erlang's guard restriction: a guard may only call a fixed set of BIFs and operators. `lfe_lint.erl` rejects anything else with `illegal guard expression`, and its `check_gexpr/4` permits a qualified call in exactly one case — module `erlang`, a function in the guard BIF set, at an arity that set allows. Every other module-qualified call in a guard is a compile error, not a style question. Verified against LFE 2.2.0 on OTP 27.3.4.15: `(lists:member x '(1 2))` and `(call 'lists 'member x '(1 2))` in a guard both fail to compile, while `(erlang:is_atom x)` compiles cleanly.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a module-qualified call in a `when` guard that Erlang does not permit"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "LFE matches clauses in order, so a clause written after one that matches everything can never run. The compiler says so itself: LFE 2.2.0 on OTP 27.3.4.15 reports \"this clause cannot match because a previous clause always matches\" for `case`, `receive` and matching `defun` alike. A catch-all is `_`, or a bare variable — LFE binds a fresh variable in a pattern rather than comparing against an existing binding, so a bare name always matches whatever else is in scope."
              },
              "id": "lfe-clause-after-catch-all",
              "properties": {
                "category": "dead-code",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "LFE matches clauses in order, so a clause written after one that matches everything can never run. The compiler says so itself: LFE 2.2.0 on OTP 27.3.4.15 reports \"this clause cannot match because a previous clause always matches\" for `case`, `receive` and matching `defun` alike. A catch-all is `_`, or a bare variable — LFE binds a fresh variable in a pattern rather than comparing against an existing binding, so a bare name always matches whatever else is in scope.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a clause that can never run because an earlier clause always matches"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Python runs the first `except` handler whose type the raised exception is an instance of, and Hy's `try` compiles to exactly that. A clause naming a supertype — or naming the same type twice, or a bare `(except [] …)` — therefore shadows every later clause it covers, which then never runs for any input. `pylint` reports the same two shapes as `E0701` and `W0705`."
              },
              "id": "hy-unreachable-except-clause",
              "properties": {
                "category": "conditions",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Python runs the first `except` handler whose type the raised exception is an instance of, and Hy's `try` compiles to exactly that. A clause naming a supertype — or naming the same type twice, or a bare `(except [] …)` — therefore shadows every later clause it covers, which then never runs for any input. `pylint` reports the same two shapes as `E0701` and `W0705`.",
                "severity": "error",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an except clause whose exception type an earlier clause in the same try already catches"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Fennel's operators compile to Lua's binary operators, not to variadic function calls, and Lua truncates a multiple-value expression to a single value everywhere except the final argument of a real call. So `(+ 1 (table.unpack [2 3 4]))` compiles to `(1 + table.unpack({2, 3, 4}))` and evaluates to 3, not 10 — the 3 and the 4 are discarded with no error. `fennel-ls` ships this as `bad-unpack`."
              },
              "id": "fennel-bad-unpack",
              "properties": {
                "category": "suspicious",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Fennel's operators compile to Lua's binary operators, not to variadic function calls, and Lua truncates a multiple-value expression to a single value everywhere except the final argument of a real call. So `(+ 1 (table.unpack [2 3 4]))` compiles to `(1 + table.unpack({2, 3, 4}))` and evaluates to 3, not 10 — the 3 and the 4 are discarded with no error. `fennel-ls` ships this as `bad-unpack`.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "an `unpack` call in the last argument of a Fennel operator, which drops every value but the first"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "These five operators are variadic and exactly associative, so `(and foo (and bar baz))` and `(and foo bar baz)` compute the same value by the same short-circuit order. The nested spelling is usually left over from an edit that added a term. `fennel-ls` ships this as `nested-associative-operator`."
              },
              "id": "fennel-nested-associative-operator",
              "properties": {
                "category": "suspicious",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "These five operators are variadic and exactly associative, so `(and foo (and bar baz))` and `(and foo bar baz)` compute the same value by the same short-circuit order. The nested spelling is usually left over from an edit that added a term. `fennel-ls` ships this as `nested-associative-operator`.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a Fennel `and`/`or`/`..`/`band`/`bor` call nested directly inside a call to itself"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`fn`, `let`, `when`, `each`, `for`, `while` and friends already evaluate every form in their body in order, so wrapping the last one in `do` adds a level of nesting and no meaning. `fennel-ls` ships this as `redundant-do`."
              },
              "id": "fennel-redundant-do",
              "properties": {
                "category": "dead-code",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`fn`, `let`, `when`, `each`, `for`, `while` and friends already evaluate every form in their body in order, so wrapping the last one in `do` adds a level of nesting and no meaning. `fennel-ls` ships this as `redundant-do`.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a `(do …)` in the tail of a Fennel form that already sequences its body"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Janet's compiler folds a constant condition and compiles the losing branch into a throwaway scope, reporting `dead code, consider removing …` at its strict lint level (`compile.c`, `janetc_throwaway`). Strict lints are off unless asked for, so the warning normally reaches nobody. In Janet only `nil` and `false` are false, so `0`, `\"\"` and `:kw` are all truthy tests."
              },
              "id": "janet-dead-branch-on-constant-condition",
              "properties": {
                "category": "dead-code",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Janet's compiler folds a constant condition and compiles the losing branch into a throwaway scope, reporting `dead code, consider removing …` at its strict lint level (`compile.c`, `janetc_throwaway`). Strict lints are off unless asked for, so the warning normally reaches nobody. In Janet only `nil` and `false` are false, so `0`, `\"\"` and `:kw` are all truthy tests.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a Janet `if`/`if-not`/`when`/`unless` whose test is a literal, so one branch never runs"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Janet's `match` documents that \"a pattern that is a symbol will match anything\", and `_` is the same with no binding. Every clause after such a pattern is dead: `(match 99 x :first 99 :second)` evaluates to `:first` even though the later clause matches the subject exactly. The usual cause is a default clause that drifted upwards, or a pattern renamed into a bare symbol by accident."
              },
              "id": "janet-unreachable-match-clause",
              "properties": {
                "category": "dead-code",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "Janet's `match` documents that \"a pattern that is a symbol will match anything\", and `_` is the same with no binding. Every clause after such a pattern is dead: `(match 99 x :first 99 :second)` evaluates to `:first` even though the later clause matches the subject exactly. The usual cause is a default clause that drifted upwards, or a pattern renamed into a bare symbol by accident.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a Janet `match` clause written after a catch-all pattern, which can never be reached"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A destructive sequence function may reuse its argument's storage to build the result, so only the **return value** names the finished sequence. Discarding it leaves the variable pointing into the middle of the old structure: SBCL 2.6.0 leaves `xs` as `(4 5)` after a discarded `(sort xs #'<)` on `(5 4 3 2 1)`. The heads here are the ones SBCL does *not* warn about — it already reports `nreverse`, `delete` and nine others."
              },
              "id": "discarded-destructive-sequence-result",
              "properties": {
                "category": "dead-code",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A destructive sequence function may reuse its argument's storage to build the result, so only the **return value** names the finished sequence. Discarding it leaves the variable pointing into the middle of the old structure: SBCL 2.6.0 leaves `xs` as `(4 5)` after a discarded `(sort xs #'<)` on `(5 4 3 2 1)`. The heads here are the ones SBCL does *not* warn about — it already reports `nreverse`, `delete` and nine others.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a destructive sequence call whose result is discarded, on a variable the body then reads"
              }
            },
