                "rationale": "`cl.el` and `cl-compat.el` ship from `lisp/obsolete/` and announce themselves as deprecated on load. They exist only to define the unprefixed aliases that `cl-lib` replaced with `cl-`-prefixed names.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a require of the obsolete cl package, which prints a deprecation notice when loaded"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A namestring assembled with `concatenate` or `format` picks a separator the program has no business choosing, and the result is then re-parsed as the host's pathname syntax — so a component containing `*` or `?` silently becomes a wild pathname and `open` signals a file-error. `merge-pathnames` and `make-pathname` compose the components directly, and the host applies its own syntax once."
              },
              "id": "pathname-built-by-concatenation",
              "properties": {
                "category": "portability",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A namestring assembled with `concatenate` or `format` picks a separator the program has no business choosing, and the result is then re-parsed as the host's pathname syntax — so a component containing `*` or `?` silently becomes a wild pathname and `open` signals a file-error. `merge-pathnames` and `make-pathname` compose the components directly, and the host applies its own syntax once.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a filesystem call whose designator is a string glued together around a path separator"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`:if-exists` defaults to `:error` for any pathname whose version is not `:newest`, which is every pathname on a host without file versions. So an output form that omits it works while the file is absent and signals `file-error` once it is not — a failure that first appears on the second run, or in production."
              },
              "id": "output-stream-without-if-exists",
              "properties": {
                "category": "suspicious",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`:if-exists` defaults to `:error` for any pathname whose version is not `:newest`, which is every pathname on a host without file versions. So an output form that omits it works while the file is absent and signals `file-error` once it is not — a failure that first appears on the second run, or in production.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a file opened for output with no :if-exists, so an existing file signals file-error"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A pathname component keeps the case its namestring was written in, and CLHS 19.2.2.1.2 leaves the host's customary case to the host. `string=` and `equal` are case-sensitive, so a test against a lowercase literal silently fails for `FOO.LISP` — and `:case :common` inverts the case rather than normalizing it, so asking for it does not help. `string-equal` is the case-insensitive comparison."
              },
              "id": "pathname-component-compared-case-sensitively",
              "properties": {
                "category": "suspicious",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A pathname component keeps the case its namestring was written in, and CLHS 19.2.2.1.2 leaves the host's customary case to the host. `string=` and `equal` are case-sensitive, so a test against a lowercase literal silently fails for `FOO.LISP` — and `:case :common` inverts the case rather than normalizing it, so asking for it does not help. `string-equal` is the case-insensitive comparison.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a pathname component compared with string=/equal against a literal, which the host's case defeats"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`directory` returns the truenames of the files its pathspec matches. A pathspec naming a directory with no wild component matches exactly one file — that directory — so the call returns a one-element list containing the argument rather than its contents, and does so without any error."
              },
              "id": "directory-without-wild-component",
