            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A process filter receives whatever bytes Emacs read, not a message. Output is delivered in chunks of at most `read-process-output-max` (65536 by default), so a record longer than one chunk — or merely one that straddles a chunk boundary — reaches the filter split in two. Handing that argument straight to a parser or a record splitter silently corrupts every record on a boundary. The manual's prescription is to append the chunk to a buffer or a process property first, then extract complete records from the accumulator."
              },
              "id": "elisp-process-filter-assumes-whole-output",
              "properties": {
                "category": "suspicious",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "A process filter receives whatever bytes Emacs read, not a message. Output is delivered in chunks of at most `read-process-output-max` (65536 by default), so a record longer than one chunk — or merely one that straddles a chunk boundary — reaches the filter split in two. Handing that argument straight to a parser or a record splitter silently corrupts every record on a boundary. The manual's prescription is to append the chunk to a buffer or a process property first, then extract complete records from the accumulator.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a process filter parsing its string argument as a complete message"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`run-with-timer`, `run-at-time` and `run-with-idle-timer` return the timer object, and `cancel-timer` takes nothing else. When the REPEAT argument is non-nil the timer fires forever, so discarding the return value leaves the program with no way to stop it — the only route back is scanning `timer-list`. A one-shot timer is self-limiting and is not reported."
              },
              "id": "elisp-repeating-timer-handle-discarded",
              "properties": {
                "category": "resource",
                "fixable": false,
                "rationale": "`run-with-timer`, `run-at-time` and `run-with-idle-timer` return the timer object, and `cancel-timer` takes nothing else. When the REPEAT argument is non-nil the timer fires forever, so discarding the return value leaves the program with no way to stop it — the only route back is scanning `timer-list`. A one-shot timer is self-limiting and is not reported.",
                "severity": "warning",
                "tags": []
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "a repeating timer whose returned handle is discarded, leaving nothing to cancel it with"
              }
            },
